    <h2>Form fields</h2>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_combo"></span> <b>Theme.</b><br> Selects the editor's visual theme.<br> The theme defines the color scheme for the background, text, syntax highlighting, and other visual elements (cursor, line numbers, selections, etc.).<br>
        Theme names are case-sensitive and must exactly match the name of the theme's CSS class. 
        Default: "default" (a light theme — [no selection]).</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Highlight brackets.</b><br> When enabled, this option highlights matching pairs of brackets whenever the cursor is placed next to one of them.<br>
        With this option active, the editor highlights the corresponding pair of brackets: (), [], or {}. The highlighting is triggered when the cursor is positioned immediately next to a bracket or between a pair of brackets—in which case the innermost pair is highlighted.<br>
        This feature only works if a mode (language definition) is loaded for the target language, as the matching logic depends on the language's grammar.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Highlight active line.</b><br> When enabled, this option highlights the line of code where the cursor is currently positioned.<br>
        This makes it easier to navigate large files and keep track of your current position while scrolling.<br>
        You can choose a default highlight style or set a custom background color for the active line.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Code folding.</b><br> When enabled, this feature allows you to hide (collapse) and show (expand) blocks of code, such as functions, classes, or conditional statements.<br>
        A small icon (an arrow or triangle) appears next to the line numbers; clicking it collapses or expands the corresponding code block.<br>
        This is particularly useful when working with large files, as it allows you to temporarily hide specific details and focus on the overall structure of the code.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Word wrap (line wrapping).</b><br> Enables automatic wrapping of lines that exceed the width of the editor.<br>
        When this option is enabled, long lines are visually broken into multiple lines, eliminating the need for horizontal scrolling. Note that no actual line-break characters are added to the code itself; this is purely a visual adjustment (soft wrap).</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Show line numbers.</b><br> When this option is enabled, line numbers are displayed along the left side of the editor.<br>
        A sequence number appears next to each line of code, making it easier to navigate the codebase, reference specific locations during discussions or debugging, and locate errors based on line numbers provided in logs or compiler messages.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Add-ons</h3>
    <p>Basic add-ons are separate JS modules that extend the functionality of the CodeMirror editor.</p>
    <ul class="g-help-list">
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Collapse XML tags.</b><br> When enabled, this allows you to hide and show blocks of XML code using buttons next to the line numbers. This is useful when working with large XML documents: you can collapse individual tags or entire branches of the structure to focus on specific sections.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Collapse Markdown code.</b><br> When enabled, this allows you to hide and show blocks in Markdown documents (such as headings, lists, quotes, or code snippets) using buttons next to the line numbers. This is helpful when working with long texts: you can collapse details to view only the document structure, then quickly expand the sections you need.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Collapse by indentation.</b><br> When enabled, this allows you to hide and show code blocks grouped by their indentation level (for example, a function body, a loop, or a conditional block) using buttons next to the line numbers. This is useful when working with languages ​​where structure is defined by indentation (such as Python or YAML), or when you need to quickly "collapse" nested constructs to get an overview of the file's overall logic.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Collapse comments.</b><br> When enabled, this option allows you to hide or show comment blocks in your code using buttons located next to the line numbers. This is helpful when working with large files: you can temporarily hide commented-out code or lengthy explanations to focus solely on the functional logic, and then quickly restore them whenever needed.</li>
        <li><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_checkbox"></span> <b>Collapse brackets.</b><br> When enabled, this option allows you to hide or show code blocks enclosed within matching brackets ((), [], {}) using buttons located next to the line numbers. This is useful when dealing with deeply nested structures: you can temporarily collapse inner blocks to view the overall structure of a function, object, or conditional statement, and then quickly expand the specific section you need.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Editor Modes</h3>
    <p><span class="g-help-fld g-help-fld_textarea"></span> Modes are a setting that tells the editor which programming language is currently being displayed, ensuring the code is highlighted correctly. You select a mode (for example, JavaScript, Python, HTML, or Markdown), and the editor immediately makes the code easier to read by using color to highlight keywords, comments, function names, and other elements. You can specify multiple modes.</p>
